WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.620
A scary grandmother figure doling out advice
and punishment feels like a familiar monster

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.099
to a lot of us.

00:00:07.099 --> 00:00:11.929
Which could be why today’s monster originally
from Russian and East Slavic folklore feels

00:00:11.929 --> 00:00:12.929
so ubiquitous.

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:18.500
She’s been the subject of operas, comic
book storylines, children’s books and cartoons,

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:24.090
and her own films—not to mention hundreds
of years of folklore history.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.660
May I present, Baba Yaga.

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:31.890
Today, she is most commonly depicted as an
ugly, evil witch—but that wasn’t always

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:32.890
her story.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:38.070
Baba Yaga was once more goddess than witch,
who reigned over time, life, death, and the

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.250
elements.

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:44.100
She was associated with strong winds and dark
forests, connected to the earth, the heavens,

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.710
and the underworld.

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:49.000
She straddles the boundary between life and
death.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.730
So which witch is it?

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:52.090
Elemental goddess?

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:53.550
Dark sorceress?

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.550
Terrifying granny?

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:57.310
Let’s find out!

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:02.050
[Monstrum intro.]

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:07.200
Associated with powerful magic and accused
of cannibalism, she has immense powers over

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.049
animals, the elements, and even time.

00:01:10.049 --> 00:01:15.890
Not all stories explicitly depict Baba Yaga
as an ugly witch—but most Russians would.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:21.470
She is portrayed as a tall, gaunt old woman:
many tales reference her “bony leg,” which

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:26.370
might be severe thinness, or it might be a
literal skeleton leg.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:31.189
She has disheveled hair and a sometimes iron
nose that is so long she can lie down on the

00:01:31.189 --> 00:01:34.470
floor of her hut and it could touch the ceiling.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:39.240
That large nose allows her to identify her
visitors by scent, leading some folklorists

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:42.090
to argue that she’s actually blind.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:46.780
The powerful Yaga always lives somewhere remote,
usually in a deep forest.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:52.430
Her home, while occasionally a grand mansion,
is most often a hut supported by chicken legs,

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:58.520
goat legs, ram horns, or spindles, and is
surrounded by a fence made from human bones

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:03.479
with the lock on her gate made from the sharp
teeth of a human jaw—so you can see why

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:05.979
people think she might be a cannibal.

00:02:05.979 --> 00:02:10.490
The hut is constantly spinning, and will only
stop with the chant: “Little house, little

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:11.640
house.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.080
Turn your back to the forest, your front to
me.”

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:19.010
Baba Yaga’s possessions are just as mystical—and
complex.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:24.380
No flying broom for this witch: the Baba Yaga
travels on a magical flying mortar, using

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:30.090
a pestle to urge it on, sweeping away the
path of her flight... with a broom.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.550
She owns enchanted mirrors that turn into
lakes, combs that can transform into forests,

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:38.990
handkerchiefs that become rivers, a flying
carpet, and fire-breathing horses.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:43.620
Based on the etymology of her name, Baba Yaga
probably dates back to at least the Medieval

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:44.660
period.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:50.260
And there are literally thousands of Baba
Yaga stories in Russian and Slavic folklore.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:55.740
The first documented evidence of Baba emerges
in woodblock prints from the late 17th century

00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:01.640
and early 18th century indicating she was
a known figure in their early oral traditions.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:06.390
The first written mention of the Baba Yaga
is in the 1755 notes of a Russian grammar

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:10.800
book penned by poet and scientist Mikhail
[Mi-kha-il] V. Lomonosov [Luh-muh-noh-suf];

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.480
he referred to ‘Iaga Bab’a twice, including
in a list comparing Roman gods with their

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:20.690
Slavic and Russian correspondents—although
she is given no counterpart.

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:25.990
The first narrative account of the Baba Yaga
is in Vasilii Levshin’s 1780 collection

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:31.670
Russian Fairy Tales, in which she is depicted
as terrifying and dangerous, with teeth like

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:34.360
tusks and hands like bear claws.

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:39.980
Until the 19th century, Baba Yaga was a largely
Eastern European monster, but she gained international

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:45.040
recognition when translations of Aleksandr Afanasyev’s

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:52.420
1855-1863 serial publications of 600 transcribed
Russian folktales appeared in the 1870s.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:56.850
His collection was styled like that of the
famed Brothers Grimm, though the inclusion

00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:01.590
of his sources and the places where he heard
the stories lends greater scholarly legitimacy

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:02.610
to his work.

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:06.230
Later, a Russian illustrator used this collection to create some of the

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:13.210
most famous renderings of Russian folk characters—including
this one of the Baba Yaga in 1899.

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:17.989
She can be altruistic, giving the protagonist
of the story gifts or advice.

00:04:17.989 --> 00:04:22.610
And even in the scariest stories, when she’s
a child kidnapper, a warrior witch, or a cruel

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:28.340
trickster, the protagonist always escapes,
and despite her iron teeth and associations

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:33.360
with cannibalism, no one in Baba Yaga stories
is ever eaten.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.870
Many Baba Yaga stories feature a young protagonist
just entering into adulthood or marriage.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:42.650
In one such story, a father gives his daughter
to Baba Yaga as a servant.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:47.610
The daughter completes her assigned chores
perfectly, with the help of little mice.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:51.120
Impressed by her work, Baba Yaga rewards the
girl with beautiful clothing.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:55.810
When the father checks to see if his daughter
is alive, he is surprised to find her “very

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:56.810
rich.”

00:04:56.810 --> 00:05:00.870
He takes her home, but her stepmother (of
course there’s an evil stepmother) is jealous,

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:05.770
so she sends her own daughter to Baba Yaga—but
this girl chases the mice with a rolling pin

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:10.909
and doesn’t do any of the work required
of her so Baba Yaga breaks her into pieces

00:05:10.909 --> 00:05:13.160
and puts her bones in a box.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:16.449
Not exactly evil, not quite benevolent there
Baba Yaga.

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:21.880
Then there’s one of the most beloved of
Russian folktales--“Vasilisa the Beautiful.”

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:26.730
On her deathbed, a merchant’s wife gives
a doll to their only daughter,Vasilisa, telling

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:29.569
her that she must always keep the doll with
her.

00:05:29.569 --> 00:05:34.159
If anything bad should happen to the girl,
she is to give the doll food and ask its advice,

00:05:34.159 --> 00:05:35.750
and the doll will help her.

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:40.240
Eventually, Vasilisa’s father remarries
a widowed woman with daughters of her own;

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:44.430
and just like in the story of Cinderella,
Vasilisa’s new stepmother and sisters are

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:48.740
far from kind, jealous of her goodness and
her beauty.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:52.680
They do all they can to diminish her allure
with hard physical work.

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:57.610
To their dismay, Vasilisa grows healthier
and more lovely, while the evil women grow

00:05:57.610 --> 00:05:59.349
uglier from their spite.

00:05:59.349 --> 00:06:03.030
But how does the girl flourish despite their
cruel actions?

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:04.580
The doll.

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:08.870
Every night Vasilisa secretly feeds the dolls
and asks its advice.

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:14.021
The doll comforts her and speaks wise words—and
the next day it does all of the girl’s work

00:06:14.021 --> 00:06:15.230
for her.

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:19.949
When it is time for Vasilisa to marry, all
the bachelors pine for her hand, and the jealous

00:06:19.949 --> 00:06:22.729
stepmother grows even more enraged.

00:06:22.729 --> 00:06:27.340
She moves them all to a new home near the
dense forest where Baba Yaga lives.

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:32.400
One night, Vasilisa and her stepsisters are
working by candlelight when a stepsister purposefully

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.072
snuffs out the light 

00:06:34.072 --> 00:06:38.599
, and then instructs Vasilisa to go
to Baba Yaga’s hut for a new flame.

00:06:38.599 --> 00:06:42.360
Scared of being eaten by Baba Yaga, Vasilisa
consults her doll.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:47.120
The doll’s eyes glow and it says that as
long as it is with her, she will be safe.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:50.029
So Vasilisa sets off for Baba Yaga’s hut.

00:06:50.029 --> 00:06:54.870
Once in the woods, she is approached in turns
by three horsemen: first, a man dressed in

00:06:54.870 --> 00:07:00.490
white atop a white steed and accompanied by
daybreak; next, a man in red riding a red

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:01.490
horse blls that still have their eyes.

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:02.490
At that moment, a man clad in black, with
an ebony stallion seeminehind whom the sun

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:07.990
rises.Vasilisa approaches a fence made of
human bones topped with skug to beckon the

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:13.289
oncoming night sky disappears at Baba Yaga’s
gate made of men’s legs.

00:07:13.289 --> 00:07:16.919
The bolts are arms, and the lock is a mouth
with sharp teeth.

00:07:16.919 --> 00:07:21.360
The eyes in the skulls begin to shine, lighting
the clearing as if it were day.

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:23.930
She has arrived at Baba Yaga’s hut.

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:27.990
Suddenly there is a horrible roar and the
terrifying woman bursts out of the forest

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:29.259
[riding in her mortar].

00:07:29.259 --> 00:07:34.050
Bravely, Vasilisa bows deeply, asking for
a light as her stepsisters had instructed.

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:37.979
The Baba Yaga says she will give Vasilisa
her light if she first does some work for

00:07:37.979 --> 00:07:40.629
her—or she will eat her.

00:07:40.629 --> 00:07:45.560
She gives Vasilisa a series of challenges:
prepare a feast for 12, clean the courtyard

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:49.469
and house, prepare wheat, wash linen, and
sweep.

00:07:49.469 --> 00:07:53.469
Vasilisa feeds her doll and it does everything
but prepare the feast.

00:07:53.469 --> 00:07:58.169
Baba Yaga is surprised that someone can do
the tasks, and do them well.

00:07:58.169 --> 00:08:02.139
Satisfied that the girl has fulfilled her
side of the bargain, she sends the girl home

00:08:02.139 --> 00:08:05.240
with one of the skulls with blazing eyes.

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:10.280
Vasilisa returns to her stepmother and stepsisters
bearing the skull like a candle, and they

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:13.839
gratefully receive her having been without
fire for days.

00:08:13.839 --> 00:08:17.750
But flames shoot from the skull’s eyes and
burn them to cinders.

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:23.229
All except Vasilisa of course, who ends up
weaving linen so fine she catches the eye

00:08:23.229 --> 00:08:26.039
of the Tsar, eventually becoming his bride.

00:08:26.039 --> 00:08:29.900
So Baba Yaga does have a heart, but you have
to earn it.

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:34.930
The story demonstrates Baba Yaga’s power
over Morning, Day, and Night—the three horsemen

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:38.430
Vasilisa had seen and whom the Baba Yaga commands.

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:43.000
It also gives us an idea of what purpose the
Baba Yaga tales might serve.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:46.779
You could argue that the experience teaches
the female audience how to be a wife—to

00:08:46.779 --> 00:08:50.600
cook, clean, and sew at another person’s
command.

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:55.259
Not all of the Baba Yaga stories end in marriage,
but they all show an encounter with the old

00:08:55.259 --> 00:08:59.190
woman as an initiation from childhood into
adult life.

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:04.380
Her tests and tasks for the protagonists assess
their cleverness and worthiness as adults.

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:09.270
She plays a cultural role as domestic instructor,
demonstrating to young women and men what

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:14.019
moving to a new household is like, and what
tasks they need to master to become eligible

00:09:14.019 --> 00:09:15.410
husbands and wives.

00:09:15.410 --> 00:09:19.889
Here’s where we can further examine the
origins of the name Baba Yaga.

00:09:19.889 --> 00:09:23.320
Her name might even hint at her possible origins
as a deity.

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:26.769
While there are variations, the word ‘baba’

00:09:26.769 --> 00:09:31.880
originally referred to a married peasant woman
of childbearing age or older, and in Old Russian

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:35.920
it was used for midwives, sorceresses, and
fortune-tellers.

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:41.839
In modern usage, baba is a derogatory term
designating a difficult old woman, which could

00:09:41.839 --> 00:09:44.460
be connected to the image of the Baba Yaga.

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:49.670
In a few indigenous Eastern European languages,
“baba’ is also the name for “pelican.”

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:54.370
Maybe that explains the Baba Yaga’s long,
beaklike nose and her hut’s chicken legs.

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:59.340
‘Yaga’ is harder to pin down, and there
isn’t a scholarly consensus on the word’s

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:00.340
etymology.

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:04.370
Some say it meant “horrible” or “horrifying”
at one point, while others connect it to the

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:06.579
Russian verb meaning “to ride.”

00:10:06.579 --> 00:10:10.209
Or we may find it’s origins in the early
Latin for ‘snake’ [anguis).

00:10:10.209 --> 00:10:14.270
But ‘Yaga’ is most commonly interpreted
as “bába-witch,” “old granny witch”

00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:15.270
or “evil baba.”

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:20.279
If we look at the variants of her name that
use ‘iaga,’ there are more theories including

00:10:20.279 --> 00:10:25.100
association with words for disease, illness
(jeza/jedza), horror (jeza), or rage (iagat).

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:30.000
That said, many names for ancient Slavic deities
have been lost over time, and this may be

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.930
the case for Baba Yaga.

00:10:31.930 --> 00:10:37.240
Perhaps the moniker is a substitute for her
original name that was once so sacred or feared

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:42.760
(likely both) that it could not be said aloud
and is now lost to history.

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:47.089
Attempts to explain Baba Yaga in the 18th
century actually make this association with

00:10:47.089 --> 00:10:52.579
ancient Slavic deities as well, including
early indications that she was a pagan goddess

00:10:52.579 --> 00:10:57.790
After all, she is associated with supernatural
powers and magical gifts and has power over

00:10:57.790 --> 00:11:00.890
life and death…Godlike traits, no?

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:03.209
Except for that whole chicken footed hut deal.

00:11:03.209 --> 00:11:08.019
But there might be a more rational explanation
for the appearance of Baba Yaga’s house.

00:11:08.019 --> 00:11:14.180
Nomadic hunters in Siberia built storehouses
on top of tree stumps to deter animal foraging.

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:18.839
Structures like the Swedish Sami storehouse
and British saddle stones are similar in form

00:11:18.839 --> 00:11:23.500
and function, and may explain why Baba Yaga’s
hut was on “chicken legs.”

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:28.860
And, as I always want to know, what was the
most common burial practice in Neolithic Central

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:29.860
Europe?

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:35.319
Bodies were placed on wooden platforms raised
up on poles so the corpses could dry out and

00:11:35.319 --> 00:11:40.500
the bones preserved—which calls to mind
depictions of Baba Yaga’s hut as well.

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:44.890
That certainly associates her with death,
as do all those bones surrounding her home,

00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:47.310
and well—the threatening to eat people.

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:49.899
The mortar and pestle that Baba Yaga uses
to get around?

00:11:49.899 --> 00:11:55.220
They were essential tools for the everyday
tasks women were in charge of in Slavic culture,

00:11:55.220 --> 00:11:57.800
like preparing food and grain, and making
medicine.

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:01.930
A lot of folklorists argue that they have
a sexual symbolism as well, but I’ll let

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:03.040
you Google that.

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:08.000
In modern depictions, Baba Yaga began to serve
as a tool for Socialist propaganda.

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:12.589
The heroes and heroines in these Baba Yaga
tales carryover from the folklore because

00:12:12.589 --> 00:12:15.980
of problem solving, hard work, and courage
they exhibit.

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:20.721
These were also traits the former Soviet Union
desired its citizens to have and Baba Yaga

00:12:20.721 --> 00:12:24.400
was a popular character on film and television.

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:29.800
But Baba Yaga found global recognition after
the fall of the Soviet Union in 1991, when

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:30.800
media produced there could be reached by other
parts of the world.

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:34.890
And since then, the Baba Yaga appeared in
the Hellboy comics and movies, television

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:39.350
shows like Lost Girl, The Magicians, Supernatural,
and—Arthur?

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:41.890
She’s even in Fortnite.

00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:48.200
Mother, bone-breaker, helper, goddess, witch—Baba
Yaga is all of these things.

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:52.930
Telling stories was an essential part of life
for the East Slavic and Russian peoples, whose

00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:59.089
folklore was popular in all social classes;
it was customary to tell skazka—folktales—at

00:12:59.089 --> 00:13:03.899
feasts and weddings, as well as share them
during times of work or rest at home.

00:13:03.899 --> 00:13:08.449
And Baba Yaga was, and still is, one of the
most popular characters.

00:13:08.449 --> 00:13:14.190
As grandmotherly advisor or cannibalistic
witch, pagan earth goddess Baba Yaga is a

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:17.880
paradox, which might be why she’s so fascinating—and
even beloved.

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:22.610
I’m going to quote Andreas Johns, who wrote
this incredibly thorough book

00:13:22.610 --> 00:13:28.610
on Baba Yaga: “It is clear that there can
be no single correct interpretation or understanding

00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:30.279
of Baba Yaga.”

00:13:30.279 --> 00:13:32.890
And there’s actually a great freedom in
that.

00:13:32.890 --> 00:13:38.509
Both for us as we conjure whichever version
of Baba Yaga that suits us best, and for the

00:13:38.509 --> 00:14:00.350
legendary woman and her own powerful potential.

